516 0 obj
<<
/Title (Theline between low-code and traditional development continues to blur. Low-code platforms are)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 452 0 R
/Next 517 0 R
>>
endobj
517 0 obj
<<
/Title (adding support for custom code extensions, version control, and CI/CD pipelines, narrowing the customization gap with traditional development. At the same time, traditional development is absorbing productivity gains from AI-assisted coding tools, component libraries, and cloud-managed infrastructure, narrowing the speed gap with low-code. Rather than one approach displacing the other, the likely trajectory is convergence: teams will choose the right level of abstraction for each part of a system instead of committing an entire organization to one development philosophy.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 452 0 R
/Prev 516 0 R
/First 592 0 R
/Last 592 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Title (Low-code and traditional development address different points on the trade-off curve between speed and control. Low-code accelerates delivery and lowers the technical barrier to building software, at the cost of flexibility and platform independence. Traditional development preserves control and scalability, at the cost of time and specialized resources. The right choice depends on project complexity, performance requirements, budget, in-house technical capacity, and the long-term strategic value of the application. Organizations that assess these factors upfront and remain open to a hybrid strategy are best positioned to choose the approach that fits each project rather than defaulting to a single methodology across the board.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 453 0 R
>>
endobj
